const timeline = [
  '15:50 - South China on-duty team reported dynamic service alarms on the IP monitoring platform.',
  '15:50 - Logged in to 4506C and 4506D switches after several slow access attempts.',
  '15:57 - Shut down port G2/6 on 4506D to isolate the suspected Layer 2 loop.',
  '16:04 - Re-enabled port G2/6 after CPU utilization and MAC flapping returned to normal.',
  '16:05 - Confirmed all affected services had recovered.',
  '18:00 - Completed extended monitoring with no further abnormal events detected.',
];
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Network incident case study for MAC flapping and HSRP instability on Cisco core switches."
    />
    <meta name="generator" content={Astro.generator} />
    <title>MAC Flapping and HSRP Instability | Dongxu Allen Hu</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="site-header">
      <a class="brand" href="/">Dongxu (Allen) Hu</a>
      <nav aria-label="Main navigation">
        <a href="/#experience">Experience</a>
        <a href="/#projects">Case Studies</a>
        <a href="/photography/">Photography</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <main>
      <section class="case-hero">
        <p class="eyebrow">Network Incident Case</p>
        <h1>MAC Flapping and HSRP Instability on Core Switches</h1>
        <p>
          A production troubleshooting case from the South China IDC environment involving MAC address flapping,
          frequent HSRP state transitions, 99% CPU utilization, and service access degradation.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="case-grid">
        <article class="case-card primary">
          <p class="eyebrow">Incident Description</p>
          <p>
            At 15:50 on June 23, the South China and North China monitoring systems reported alarms for dynamic service
            access issues in the South China region.
          </p>
          <p>
            I immediately logged in to the 4506C and 4506D switches. On both switches, I found a large number of MAC
            address flapping logs and frequent HSRP state transitions. The CPU utilization on both switches had reached
            99%.
          </p>
          <p>
            After the incident occurred, multiple departments reported that the IP addresses of the affected services
            became slow to access or completely unreachable.
          </p>
        </article>

        <aside class="case-card impact">
          <p class="eyebrow">Impact</p>
          <strong>Dynamic service access degradation</strong>
          <span>Core switch CPU reached 99%</span>
          <span>MAC address flapping and HSRP instability</span>
          <span>Services slow or unreachable</span>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section class="case-card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Actions Taken</p>
        <ol class="timeline-list">
          {timeline.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
        </ol>
        <p>
          Following the company's incident handling process, I immediately contacted the relevant supervisor by phone
          and reported the troubleshooting steps I was about to take. Based on the symptoms, I determined that the issue
          was likely caused by a Layer 2 loop. After port G2/6 on 4506D was shut down, CPU utilization on both switches
          quickly dropped back to normal, and the MAC address flapping issue disappeared.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="case-grid">
        <article class="case-card">
          <p class="eyebrow">Root Cause Analysis</p>
          <p>
            Based on the incident symptoms, the large number of MAC address flapping events was likely caused by an STP
            failure, which resulted in a Layer 2 loop.
          </p>
          <p>
            When 4506C, 4506D, and the access-layer switches formed a Layer 2 loop, a large number of Layer 2 broadcast
            packets circulated repeatedly between the switches. This consumed significant CPU and bandwidth resources on
            the core switches, causing related services to become slow or unreachable.
          </p>
          <p>
            After reviewing the logs prior to the first MAC address flapping event, no clear abnormal log was found that
            could directly explain the STP failure. Therefore, the preliminary conclusion was that an STP software
            failure may have triggered the incident.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="case-card">
          <p class="eyebrow">Response and Optimization</p>
          <h2>Emergency Response Plan</h2>
          <p>The incident was handled according to the company's Network Access System Incident Response Procedure.</p>
          <h2>Follow-up Optimization Plan</h2>
          <p>
            To reduce the risk of similar incidents happening again, I recommended enabling UDLD and STP loop protection
            mechanisms on all Layer 2 links in the South China IDC.
          </p>
          <p>
            I also drafted an implementation plan for preventing Layer 2 loops, titled:
            <strong>Solution for Preventing Layer 2 Loop Incidents</strong>.
          </p>
        </article>
      </section>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>

